<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tobacco">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tobacco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nicotiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tobacco is the common name of plants of the genus Nicotiana and of any product prepared from their cured leaves. It has long been used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, with some cultivation sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> dating to 1400 to 1000 BCE. Many Native American peoples grew and used it, smoking it socially and ceremonially, as to seal a peace treaty. In 1559 Francisco Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> was the first European to bring tobacco seeds to the Old World. Production for smoking, chewing and snuff had become a major industry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and its colonies by 1700. Tobacco use is now recognised as a cause of cancer and other diseases.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tobacco.jpg" alt="Tobacco" width="330" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jom / Joachim Müllerchen · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabak_9290019.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
